# Keep the build context small and secrets out of the image. The Dockerfile uses
# COPY . ., so secret-file patterns are depth-matched (**/) to keep any .gitignored
# local secret — at any level — out of the build context and its cache.
.git
.github
# Parallel-session worktrees: each is a whole extra checkout under the repo
# root, and compose builds with context ../.., so without this every live
# worktree is swept into the build context. The secret patterns below already
# depth-match, so nothing leaked — this is about context size and stale
# artifacts (coverage.out below is root-only).
.claude/worktrees
**/.env
**/.env.*
# google-github-actions/auth writes its federated credential into the workspace
# as gha-creds-*.json, and the delivery workflow's `docker build .` has the
# workspace as its context. The multi-stage build means it never reaches the
# pushed image — only /out binaries are copied forward — but it would otherwise
# sit in the build stage's layer and its cache for the life of the job.
**/gha-creds-*.json
**/*.local.yaml
**/*.local.yml
**/model-providers.json
deploy/helm
deploy/compose
*.md
coverage.out
*.cov
# Host build outputs at the repo root, the same set .gitignore now covers. Git
# ignoring them keeps them out of commits; this keeps them out of the build
# context, which is a separate path — a developer who runs `go build -o worker
# ./cmd/worker` still has the file on disk, and COPY . . would ship ~80 MB of
# host-arch binary into the image and its cache. Root-only, like coverage.out.
/brain
/changelog
/controlplane
/executor
/gate
/worker
